  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 310 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Continued dry with seasonable temperatures \nthrough the middle of next week.\n\nGOES water vapor channels depict a highly amplified upper ridge\nthat extends from the central US northward toward James Bay in \nCanada. This ridge will be strong enough to keep the upstream \ntroughing over the Desert SW from getting east of the Plains \nstates through the middle of next week. Meanwhile, light \nnortherly flow aloft will continue across the local area through\nSunday, before weak shortwave energy passes through and moves \noff the SE coast Sunday night, which will allow the longwave \npattern to dampen to some extent with the ridge becoming broader\nand flatter with the axis in vicinity of the Great Lakes. At \nthe surface, 1035mb high pressure is centered over eastern PA \nand NJ this afternoon. Partly cloudy with SCT to BKN \ncumulus/stratocumulus. Temperatures are in the lower to mid 60s \nwith a NE wind of 5-10 mph inland, and 10-15 mph with gusts up \nto 20 mph toward the coast.\n\nThe high continues to gradually build south and into the local \narea tonight. For tonight into early Sunday morning, \ntemperatures should be slightly lower with mid 30s NW and upper \n30s to lower 40s for most of central and south-central VA. \nPatchy frost is expected for areas to the N and W of Richmond, \nand interior portions of Dorchester and Wicomico counties in MD.\nHowever, coverage and overall threat is not quite enough to \nwarrant a Frost Advisory at this time. As the high continues to \nsettle in from the north Sunday, cloud cover should be less, but\na period of SCT-BKN cumulus are still likely along and S of \nI-64. Temperatures warm a few degrees with highs in the mid 60s \nto near 70 F. Sunday night/early Monday morning will likely see \nthe greatest potential for a more widespread frost spreading \neast from the piedmont to interior SE VA as better decoupling is\nexpected. Continued the same thinking as the previous shift to \ngo a few degrees (or several degrees in some areas) colder than \nNBM, with mid 30s forecast for much of the piedmont and and mid-\nupper 30s for interior SE VA.\n\nWarmer Monday ahead of the next (dry) front, then slightly \ncooler into midweek and staying dry. The 24/12z ECMWF \nensemble/GEFS chance for just .01\" of rain through mid week is \nbasically none, with some low probs showing up in southeastern \nportions of the area later in the week into the following \nweekend, but when switching the threshold to 0.1\" there are \npractically no probs through the end of the month.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Elevated tidal anomalies continue tonight into \nSunday.\n\nCoastal Flood Advisories continue until 4 PM for portions of \nthe bay side of the MD Eastern Shore as well as the south bank \nof the Potomac. Tonight's tide cycle will be the lower of the \ntwo upcoming high tides. Expecting mostly nuisance-level \nflooding tonight, though Lewisetta and Bishops Head will come \nclose to minor thresholds. Persistent NE winds near the mouth of\nthe bay, along with slowly decreasing swells have resulted in \nonly a slow decrease in tidal anomalies, especially for the \nmiddle and upper Ches Bay. Sunday's high tide will likely exceed\nminor flood thresholds in some of these areas. Additional \nCoastal Flood Advisories are possible as increasing astronomical\ntides counteract decreasing anomalies.",
